// Class 9 Mathematics practice bank (Ganita Manjari), part 2: Part II chapters (sequences to surface area and volume).
StudyData.addPractice(9, "maths", {
  "predicting-what-comes-next-exploring-sequences-and-progressions": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "In the Virahānka–Fibonacci sequence 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13, …, the next term is", options: ["20", "21", "26", "18"], answer: 1, explain: "Each term is the sum of the previous two: 8 + 13 = 21.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The common ratio of the geometric progression 5, 15, 45, … is", options: ["5", "10", "3", "30"], answer: 2, explain: "Each term is 3 times the one before: 15 ÷ 5 = 3 and 45 ÷ 15 = 3. The number 10 is the difference of the first two terms, which has no meaning for a GP.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The sum 1 + 2 + 3 + … + 10 equals", options: ["55", "50", "45", "110"], answer: 0, explain: "n(n + 1)/2 with n = 10 gives 10 × 11/2 = 55. The value 110 is n(n + 1) without halving.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The 7th triangular number is", options: ["21", "28", "36", "49"], answer: 1, explain: "t₇ = 7 × 8/2 = 28. (Equivalently 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 + 6 + 7 = 28.)", level: 1 },
      { q: "The 15th term of the arithmetic progression 8, 5, 2, … is", options: ["−37", "34", "−31", "−34"], answer: 3, explain: "a = 8, d = −3. t₁₅ = 8 + 14 × (−3) = 8 − 42 = −34.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The 7th term of the geometric progression 2, 6, 18, … is", options: ["486", "1458", "4374", "42"], answer: 1, explain: "a = 2, r = 3, so t₇ = 2 × 3⁶ = 2 × 729 = 1458. The value 486 is the 6th term.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Rani saves ₹5 on day 1, ₹10 on day 2, ₹15 on day 3 and so on, adding ₹5 more each day.\nHer total savings after 20 days is", options: ["210", "2100", "1050", "1000"], answer: 2, explain: "The savings are 5 × (1 + 2 + … + 20) = 5 × 20 × 21/2 = 5 × 210 = ₹1050. The number 210 forgets the factor of 5.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): The sequence 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, … is neither an AP nor a GP.\nReason (R): Its consecutive differences 3, 5, 7, … are not equal, and its consecutive ratios 4, 9/4, 16/9, … are not equal.",options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 0, explain: "A sequence is an AP only if differences are equal, and a GP only if ratios are equal. R shows neither holds, so R explains A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "Assertion (A): The sequence 2, 4, 8, 16, … is an arithmetic progression.\nReason (R): Each term of the sequence is obtained by multiplying the previous term by 2.",options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 3, explain: "R is true, but it describes a geometric progression with ratio 2. The differences 2, 4, 8, … are not equal, so it is not an AP and A is false.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "The 4th term of an AP is 14 and its 9th term is 34. The 20th term is", options: ["74", "82", "78", "80"], answer: 2, explain: "5d = 34 − 14 = 20, so d = 4. a = 14 − 3 × 4 = 2. t₂₀ = 2 + 19 × 4 = 78.", level: 3 },
      { q: "In a Sierpiński triangle construction, stage 0 has 1 triangle, and at every stage each triangle is replaced by 3 smaller ones.\nThe number of small triangles at stage 5 is", options: ["15", "125", "243", "729"], answer: 2, explain: "The count follows a GP with ratio 3: 1, 3, 9, 27, 81, 243. Stage 5 has 3⁵ = 243. The number 15 would come from adding 3 each time, which is an AP.", level: 3 },
      { q: "The sum of the natural numbers from 21 to 60, both included, is", options: ["1830", "1410", "1590", "1620"], answer: 3, explain: "Sum of 1 to 60 = 60 × 61/2 = 1830. Sum of 1 to 20 = 210. So 21 to 60 gives 1830 − 210 = 1620.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Write the next two terms of the sequence 3, 6, 9, 12, …", a: "The common difference is 3, so the next terms are **15 and 18**.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Write the formula for the nth term of a GP with first term a and common ratio r.", a: "**tₙ = a rⁿ⁻¹**.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Find the 12th triangular number.", a: "t₁₂ = 12 × 13/2 = **78**.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Find the 25th term of the AP 3, 7, 11, …", a: "a = 3, d = 4. t₂₅ = 3 + 24 × 4 = 3 + 96 = **99**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Find the 5th term of the GP 3, 6, 12, … and say how you know it is a GP.", a: "The ratios 6/3 and 12/6 are both 2, so it is a GP with r = 2.\nt₅ = 3 × 2⁴ = 3 × 16 = **48**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "The triangular numbers are 1, 3, 6, 10, 15, … Is this sequence an AP? Give a reason and write the next term.", a: "The differences are 2, 3, 4, 5, …, which are not equal, so it is **not an AP**.\nThe next difference is 6, so the next term is 15 + 6 = **21**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Find the sum 1 + 2 + 3 + … + 50.", a: "S = n(n + 1)/2 with n = 50.\nS = 50 × 51/2 = **1275**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Which term of the AP 7, 13, 19, … is 139?", a: "a = 7, d = 6. Set 7 + (n − 1) × 6 = 139.\n(n − 1) × 6 = 132, so n − 1 = 22.\n**n = 23**; the 23rd term is 139.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Show that the sequence whose nth term is tₙ = 3n + 2 is an AP. Write its first term and common difference.", a: "t₁ = 5, t₂ = 8, t₃ = 11, …\ntₙ₊₁ − tₙ = [3(n + 1) + 2] − [3n + 2] = 3, which is the same for every n.\nA constant difference means it is an AP with **a = 5 and d = 3**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "The 3rd term of a GP is 12 and its 6th term is 96. Find the first term, the common ratio and the 8th term.", a: "t₆ ÷ t₃ = r³ = 96/12 = 8, so r = 2.\nt₃ = a r² = 4a = 12, so a = 3.\nt₈ = 3 × 2⁷ = 3 × 128 = **384**.\n(Check: t₆ = 3 × 32 = 96.)", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Derive a formula for the sum of the first n natural numbers. Hence find (i) 1 + 2 + … + 100 and (ii) the sum of the even numbers 2 + 4 + … + 100.", a: "Let S = 1 + 2 + … + n. Writing it in reverse, S = n + (n − 1) + … + 1.\nAdding term by term, each of the n pairs gives n + 1, so 2S = n(n + 1) and **S = n(n + 1)/2**.\n(i) 100 × 101/2 = **5050**.\n(ii) 2 + 4 + … + 100 = 2(1 + 2 + … + 50) = 2 × (50 × 51/2) = **2550**.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "The 5th term of an AP is 23 and its 12th term is 58. Find the AP, its 20th term, and the position of the term 143.", a: "7d = 58 − 23 = 35, so d = 5.\na + 4d = 23 gives a = 23 − 20 = 3.\nThe AP is **3, 8, 13, 18, …**.\nt₂₀ = 3 + 19 × 5 = **98**.\nFor 143: 3 + (n − 1) × 5 = 143, so (n − 1) = 28 and n = **29**. The 29th term is 143.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "In the Sierpiński triangle, stage 0 is a single triangle of area 1 unit. At each stage every remaining triangle is cut into 4 equal triangles and the middle one is removed, leaving 3.\n(i) How many triangles are there at stage 4?\n(ii) What is the area of each at stage 4?\n(iii) What is the total remaining area at stage 4?\n(iv) Name the type of progression the total areas form and its common ratio.", a: "(i) The count triples each stage: 3⁴ = **81** triangles.\n(ii) Each stage divides the area of one triangle by 4: (1/4)⁴ = **1/256** unit.\n(iii) Total = 81 × 1/256 = **81/256** unit (about 0.32).\n(iv) The totals 1, 3/4, 9/16, 27/64, 81/256 form a **GP with common ratio 3/4**.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: Pooja starts a running routine. She runs 600 m on day 1 and increases the distance by 200 m every day.\n(i) How far does she run on day 10?\n(ii) On which day does she first run 3000 m?\n(iii) What is the total distance she runs in the first 5 days?", a: "The distances form an AP with a = 600 and d = 200.\n(i) t₁₀ = 600 + 9 × 200 = **2400 m**.\n(ii) 600 + (n − 1) × 200 = 3000 gives (n − 1) = 12, so **n = 13**: day 13.\n(iii) The first five terms are 600, 800, 1000, 1200, 1400. Sum = 5000 m, i.e. **5 km**.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "propositions-and-their-converses": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The converse of the statement 'If P, then Q' is", options: ["If not P, then not Q", "If Q, then P", "If P, then Q", "If not Q, then P"], answer: 1, explain: "The converse swaps the condition and the conclusion: 'If Q, then P'.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A single example that satisfies the condition of a statement but not its conclusion, and so shows it is false, is called a", options: ["theorem", "proof", "counterexample", "converse"], answer: 2, explain: "A counterexample disproves a statement. One is enough, while many examples cannot prove a statement true.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which set of side lengths forms a right-angled triangle?", options: ["5, 6, 7", "4, 5, 6", "7, 8, 9", "6, 8, 10"], answer: 3, explain: "6² + 8² = 36 + 64 = 100 = 10². The other sets do not satisfy a² + b² = c² (for example 25 + 36 = 61 ≠ 49).", level: 1 },
      { q: "A counterexample to the statement 'Every prime number is odd' is", options: ["1", "2", "9", "15"], answer: 1, explain: "2 is prime but even. The numbers 9 and 15 are odd and not prime, 1 is neither prime nor composite; none of them disproves the statement.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The converse of 'If a quadrilateral is a rectangle, then its diagonals are equal' is", options: ["If a quadrilateral has equal diagonals, then it is a rectangle", "If a quadrilateral is not a rectangle, then its diagonals are not equal", "If the diagonals of a quadrilateral are unequal, then it is a rectangle", "A rectangle has equal diagonals"], answer: 0, explain: "Swap the two parts of the statement. (This converse is actually false: an isosceles trapezium has equal diagonals but is not a rectangle.)", level: 2 },
      { q: "A triangle has sides 8 cm, 15 cm and 17 cm. The right angle, if any, is opposite the side of length", options: ["8 cm", "15 cm", "17 cm", "none; it is not right-angled"], answer: 2, explain: "8² + 15² = 64 + 225 = 289 = 17². By the converse of the Pythagoras theorem the triangle is right-angled, with the right angle opposite the longest side, 17 cm.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Ravi claims: 'If a number is a multiple of 5, then its last digit is 5.'\nWhich number shows that Ravi is wrong?", options: ["15", "25", "10", "35"], answer: 2, explain: "10 is a multiple of 5 but its last digit is 0, so it satisfies the condition and fails the conclusion. 15, 25 and 35 agree with the claim.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): 'If a number is a multiple of 8, then it is a multiple of 4' is true, but its converse is false.\nReason (R): 12 is a multiple of 4 but not a multiple of 8.",options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 0, explain: "Multiples of 8 are 8k = 4(2k), so the statement is true. The converse says every multiple of 4 is a multiple of 8, and 12 is a counterexample, so R explains why the converse is false.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "Assertion (A): Checking a statement for many numbers proves that it is true for all numbers.\nReason (R): One counterexample is enough to show that a statement is false.",options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 3, explain: "R is true. A is false: Fermat's claim about 2^(2ⁿ) + 1 was true for n = 0 to 4 but fails at n = 5. A proof must cover every case.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "Which of these statements has a true converse?", options: ["If a number is divisible by 9, it is divisible by 3.", "If a quadrilateral is a square, it is a rectangle.", "If a triangle has two equal sides, it has two equal angles.", "If two numbers are even, their sum is even."], answer: 2, explain: "The converse of the third statement, 'two equal angles means two equal sides', is true. The other converses fail: 6 is divisible by 3 but not 9; a 2 × 3 rectangle is not a square; 1 + 3 = 4 is even though 1 and 3 are not.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Fermat claimed that 2^(2ⁿ) + 1 is prime for every n = 0, 1, 2, …\nThe smallest n for which this fails is", options: ["3", "4", "5", "6"], answer: 2, explain: "For n = 0 to 4 the values 3, 5, 17, 257 and 65537 are all prime. For n = 5, 2³² + 1 = 4294967297 = 641 × 6700417, which is composite (Euler's counterexample).", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which pair of right-angled triangles shows that 'If two triangles have equal areas, they are congruent' is false?", options: ["legs 3 and 4; legs 4 and 3", "legs 5 and 4; legs 5 and 4", "legs 6 and 2; legs 5 and 3", "legs 6 and 2; legs 4 and 3"], answer: 3, explain: "Legs 6 and 2 give area ½ × 6 × 2 = 6, and legs 4 and 3 give ½ × 4 × 3 = 6. The areas are equal but the triangles are not congruent. The first two pairs are congruent, and in the third pair the areas are 6 and 7.5.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Write the converse of 'If a triangle is equilateral, then it is isosceles'.", a: "'If a triangle is isosceles, then it is equilateral.' (This converse is false.)", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Give a counterexample to the statement 'All odd numbers are prime'.", a: "**9** is odd but not prime (9 = 3 × 3). Another is 15.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State the converse of the Baudhāyana–Pythagoras theorem.", a: "If in a triangle the square of one side equals the sum of the squares of the other two sides (a² + b² = c²), then the triangle is right-angled.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Is a triangle with sides 8 cm, 15 cm and 17 cm right-angled? Which angle is the right angle?", a: "8² + 15² = 64 + 225 = 289 = 17².\nBy the converse of the Pythagoras theorem the triangle is right-angled, and the right angle is **opposite the side 17 cm**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Write the converse of 'If a number is a multiple of 10, then it is a multiple of 5'. Is the converse true?", a: "Converse: 'If a number is a multiple of 5, then it is a multiple of 10.'\nIt is **false**: 15 is a multiple of 5 but not of 10.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Show that the statement 'The sum of any two prime numbers is even' is false.", a: "Take the primes 2 and 3. Their sum is 5, which is odd.\nOne counterexample is enough, so the statement is **false**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A perfect square has an odd number of factors, and a number with an odd number of factors is a perfect square. Verify the first statement for 36 and write the second statement as the converse of the first.", a: "Factors of 36: 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 9, 12, 18, 36, which is 9 factors, an odd number, and 36 = 6² is a perfect square.\nStatement: 'If a number is a perfect square, then it has an odd number of factors.'\nConverse: **'If a number has an odd number of factors, then it is a perfect square.'**", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Disprove 'If two triangles have equal areas, they are congruent'. Is its converse true?", a: "Take a right triangle with legs 6 cm and 2 cm (area 6 cm²) and another with legs 4 cm and 3 cm (area 6 cm²). Their areas are equal but they are not congruent (the sides differ). So the statement is **false**.\nConverse: 'If two triangles are congruent, they have equal areas.' This is **true**, since congruent triangles coincide.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Prove that if n is an odd integer then n² is odd. Is the converse true? Give a reason.", a: "Let n = 2k + 1 for some integer k.\nn² = 4k² + 4k + 1 = 2(2k² + 2k) + 1, which is odd.\nConverse: 'If n² is odd, then n is odd.' It is **true**: if n were even, n = 2m, then n² = 4m² would be even.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Using the converse of the Pythagoras theorem, decide which of these are right-angled triangles: (i) 9, 40, 41 (ii) 10, 24, 26 (iii) 7, 15, 17.", a: "(i) 9² + 40² = 81 + 1600 = 1681 = 41². **Right-angled.**\n(ii) 10² + 24² = 100 + 576 = 676 = 26². **Right-angled.**\n(iii) 7² + 15² = 49 + 225 = 274, but 17² = 289. **Not right-angled.**", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Prove the converse of the Baudhāyana–Pythagoras theorem: if in △ABC we have AB² + BC² = AC², then ∠B = 90°.", a: "Construct a right-angled △PQR with ∠Q = 90°, PQ = AB and QR = BC.\nBy the Pythagoras theorem, PR² = PQ² + QR² = AB² + BC².\nBut AB² + BC² = AC² (given), so PR² = AC² and PR = AC.\nNow in △ABC and △PQR: AB = PQ, BC = QR and AC = PR, so the triangles are congruent by SSS.\nHence ∠B = ∠Q = **90°**.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Write the converse of each statement and decide whether the statement and its converse are true. Give a counterexample where false.\n(i) If a number is divisible by 12, it is divisible by 3.\n(ii) If a quadrilateral is a parallelogram, its opposite sides are equal.\n(iii) If a number is divisible by 10, its last digit is 0.", a: "(i) Statement: true (12k = 3 × 4k). Converse: 'If a number is divisible by 3, it is divisible by 12': **false**; 9 is divisible by 3 but not by 12.\n(ii) Statement: **true**. Converse: 'If the opposite sides of a quadrilateral are equal, it is a parallelogram': **true**.\n(iii) Statement: **true**. Converse: 'If a number's last digit is 0, it is divisible by 10': **true**.\nSo the converse may be true or false and must be checked separately.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Fermat believed that the numbers 2^(2ⁿ) + 1 are always prime. List the values for n = 0 to 4, say how Euler disproved the claim, and explain why checking five cases was not a proof.", a: "n = 0: 2¹ + 1 = 3; n = 1: 2² + 1 = 5; n = 2: 2⁴ + 1 = 17; n = 3: 2⁸ + 1 = 257; n = 4: 2¹⁶ + 1 = 65537. All of these are prime.\nEuler showed that for n = 5, 2³² + 1 = 4294967297 = 641 × 6700417, so it is composite. This single case is a **counterexample** and disproves the claim.\nChecking five cases only shows the claim works for those values; a proof must cover every n, and one failure is enough to make a general statement false.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: Sunita says, 'If a number is divisible by 4, then it is even.' Her friend Tara says, 'The converse is also true.'\n(i) Is Sunita's statement true? Give a reason.\n(ii) Write the converse and say whether Tara is right.\n(iii) Why would checking 4, 8 and 12 not be enough to prove Sunita's statement?", a: "(i) Yes. Any multiple of 4 is 4k = 2(2k), which is even.\n(ii) Converse: 'If a number is even, then it is divisible by 4.' Tara is **wrong**: 6 is even but not divisible by 4.\n(iii) A few examples do not cover all numbers; a general argument like 4k = 2(2k) is needed to prove a statement true for all cases.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "how-quantities-combine-understanding-data": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "A student scores 70 in a test of weight 2 and 80 in a test of weight 3. The weighted mean score is", options: ["75", "76", "77", "78"], answer: 1, explain: "Weighted mean = (2 × 70 + 3 × 80)/(2 + 3) = (140 + 240)/5 = 76. The simple average, 75, ignores the weights.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The mean of 5 numbers is 12. Their sum is", options: ["17", "60", "12", "5"], answer: 1, explain: "Sum = mean × number of values = 12 × 5 = 60.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which chart makes every bar the same height so that proportions can be compared?", options: ["A stacked bar chart", "A 100% stacked bar chart", "A simple bar chart", "A line graph"], answer: 1, explain: "A 100% stacked bar chart scales each bar to 100%, so only the shares of the parts are compared.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The weighted mean becomes the ordinary mean when", options: ["the weights are all zero", "one weight is very large", "all the weights are equal", "the data are not numbers"], answer: 2, explain: "If every weight is the same, it cancels from the numerator and the denominator, leaving the usual average.", level: 1 },
      { q: "15 boys have an average of 48 marks and 25 girls have an average of 56 marks. The average of all 40 students is", options: ["52", "54", "53", "51"], answer: 2, explain: "Total = 15 × 48 + 25 × 56 = 720 + 1400 = 2120. Mean = 2120/40 = 53. The value 52 is the simple average of 48 and 56, which wrongly gives both groups equal weight.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The mean of 8 numbers is 15. When one number, 29, is removed, the mean of the other 7 numbers is", options: ["14", "15", "12", "13"], answer: 3, explain: "Sum of 8 numbers = 120. After removing 29, the sum = 91, and the mean = 91/7 = 13.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A shopkeeper mixes 3 kg of tea costing ₹200 per kg with 2 kg of tea costing ₹300 per kg.\nThe cost per kg of the mixture is", options: ["₹250", "₹240", "₹260", "₹225"], answer: 1, explain: "Total cost = 3 × 200 + 2 × 300 = 600 + 600 = ₹1200 for 5 kg, so ₹240 per kg. ₹250 is the simple average.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): A 100% stacked bar chart can be made from a stacked bar chart, but a stacked bar chart cannot be made from a 100% stacked bar chart.\nReason (R): A 100% stacked bar chart does not show the totals of the bars.",options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 0, explain: "Converting to percentages loses the totals, so the original heights cannot be recovered. R states precisely why the reverse is impossible.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "Assertion (A): The average of the averages of two classes always equals the average of all the students.\nReason (R): The two classes may have different numbers of students.",options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 3, explain: "R is true, and it shows why A fails: the larger class should count for more. Only when the classes are of equal size is the simple average of the two means correct.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "How many litres of 40% salt solution must be added to 6 litres of 15% salt solution to make a 25% solution?", options: ["5 L", "6 L", "3 L", "4 L"], answer: 3, explain: "Salt: 0.15 × 6 + 0.40x = 0.25(6 + x), so 0.9 + 0.4x = 1.5 + 0.25x, giving 0.15x = 0.6 and x = 4. Check: 2.5 L of salt in 10 L is 25%.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A course grade counts homework 20%, project 30% and exam 50%.\nA student scores 90 in homework, 70 in the project and 80 in the exam. The final grade is", options: ["80", "78", "79", "85"], answer: 2, explain: "Weighted score = 0.20 × 90 + 0.30 × 70 + 0.50 × 80 = 18 + 21 + 40 = 79.", level: 3 },
      { q: "The mean of 10 numbers is 24. It is then found that 45 was read in place of 15, and 17 in place of 37. The correct mean is", options: ["24", "23", "25", "22"], answer: 1, explain: "Wrong sum = 240. Correct sum = 240 − 45 + 15 − 17 + 37 = 230. Correct mean = 230/10 = 23.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Write the formula for the weighted mean of values x₁, x₂, …, xₙ with weights w₁, w₂, …, wₙ.", a: "Weighted mean = (w₁x₁ + w₂x₂ + … + wₙxₙ) ÷ (w₁ + w₂ + … + wₙ).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "The mean of 6 numbers is 9. What is their sum?", a: "Sum = 9 × 6 = **54**.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What does a 100% stacked bar chart show, and what does it hide?", a: "It shows the **proportion** of each part in every bar. It hides the **totals** of the bars.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Find the weighted mean of 80 (weight 2), 90 (weight 3) and 70 (weight 5).", a: "Weighted mean = (2 × 80 + 3 × 90 + 5 × 70)/(2 + 3 + 5)\n= (160 + 270 + 350)/10 = 780/10 = **78**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "The mean of 6 numbers is 18. One of the numbers, 33, is removed. Find the mean of the remaining numbers.", a: "Sum of 6 numbers = 6 × 18 = 108.\nRemaining sum = 108 − 33 = 75 for 5 numbers.\nNew mean = 75/5 = **15**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "3 kg of rice costing ₹40 per kg is mixed with 2 kg of rice costing ₹60 per kg. Find the cost per kg of the mixture.", a: "Total cost = 3 × 40 + 2 × 60 = 120 + 120 = ₹240.\nTotal weight = 5 kg.\nCost per kg = 240/5 = **₹48**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "5 litres of a 12% sugar solution is mixed with 15 litres of a 20% sugar solution. Find the concentration of the mixture.", a: "Sugar = 0.12 × 5 + 0.20 × 15 = 0.6 + 3 = 3.6 L.\nTotal volume = 20 L.\nConcentration = 3.6/20 = 0.18 = **18%**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Three classes have 30, 20 and 50 students with average marks 62, 72 and 56. Find the average mark of all 100 students.", a: "Total marks = 30 × 62 + 20 × 72 + 50 × 56 = 1860 + 1440 + 2800 = 6100.\nAverage = 6100/100 = **61**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Group A has 10 students with mean 40 and group B has 30 students with mean 70. Show that the simple average of the two means is not the mean of all 40 students.", a: "Simple average of the means = (40 + 70)/2 = 55.\nCorrect combined mean = (10 × 40 + 30 × 70)/40 = (400 + 2100)/40 = **62.5**.\n55 ≠ 62.5, because group B is three times as large and must carry more weight.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A subject's final score counts a quiz 20%, an assignment 30% and an exam 50%. Anita scores 85, 70 and 60; Bimal scores 60, 80 and 70. Who has the higher final score?", a: "Anita: 0.20 × 85 + 0.30 × 70 + 0.50 × 60 = 17 + 21 + 30 = 68.\nBimal: 0.20 × 60 + 0.30 × 80 + 0.50 × 70 = 12 + 24 + 35 = 71.\n**Bimal** has the higher final score (71 against 68), because the exam carries the greatest weight.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "The mean weight of 9 students is 40 kg. A tenth student joins and the mean becomes 42 kg. (i) Find the weight of the new student. (ii) Later a student weighing 51 kg leaves. Find the mean weight of the remaining 9 students.", a: "(i) Total weight of 9 students = 9 × 40 = 360 kg.\nTotal weight of 10 students = 10 × 42 = 420 kg.\nNew student = 420 − 360 = **60 kg**.\n(ii) After a 51 kg student leaves: total = 420 − 51 = 369 kg for 9 students.\nMean = 369/9 = **41 kg**.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "How many litres of 50% sugar syrup must be mixed with 10 litres of 20% syrup to get a 30% syrup? Find the volume of the mixture and the amount of sugar in it.", a: "Let x litres of 50% syrup be added.\nSugar in the mixture = 0.20 × 10 + 0.50x = 2 + 0.5x.\nThe mixture has (10 + x) litres at 30%, so sugar = 0.30(10 + x) = 3 + 0.3x.\nEquate: 2 + 0.5x = 3 + 0.3x, so 0.2x = 1 and **x = 5 litres**.\nVolume of the mixture = 15 litres; sugar = 0.30 × 15 = **4.5 litres**.\nCheck: 2 + 2.5 = 4.5.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "A stacked bar chart shows the number of boys and girls in three sections: 9A has 12 boys and 18 girls, 9B has 20 boys and 20 girls, 9C has 15 boys and 25 girls.\n(i) Find the total in each section.\n(ii) Find the percentage of girls in each section.\n(iii) Which section has the highest share of girls?\n(iv) Why would a 100% stacked bar chart be better for (iii), and what would it hide?", a: "(i) 9A: 30; 9B: 40; 9C: 40.\n(ii) 9A: 18/30 = **60%**; 9B: 20/40 = **50%**; 9C: 25/40 = **62.5%**.\n(iii) **9C** has the highest share of girls.\n(iv) In a 100% stacked chart all bars have the same height, so the girls' portion can be compared directly as a share. It would hide the totals (30 against 40 students).", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: Rahul's scores in five cricket innings were 40, 55, 0, 25 and 60.\n(i) Find his mean score.\n(ii) How many runs must he score in the sixth innings to raise his mean to 40?\n(iii) If he scores 0 in the sixth innings instead, what is his mean?", a: "(i) Sum = 40 + 55 + 0 + 25 + 60 = 180; mean = 180/5 = **36**.\n(ii) For a mean of 40 over 6 innings the total must be 240, so he needs 240 − 180 = **60 runs**.\n(iii) Total stays 180 over 6 innings: mean = 180/6 = **30**.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "the-world-of-algorithms": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The greatest common divisor of 12 and 18 is", options: ["3", "6", "12", "36"], answer: 1, explain: "The common divisors are 1, 2, 3 and 6; the greatest is 6. (36 is their least common multiple.)", level: 1 },
      { q: "The value of gcd(17, 0) is", options: ["0", "1", "34", "17"], answer: 3, explain: "Every number divides 0, so gcd(m, 0) = m. Hence gcd(17, 0) = 17.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The value of 29 mod 6 is", options: ["4", "5", "1", "6"], answer: 1, explain: "29 = 4 × 6 + 5, so the remainder on dividing 29 by 6 is 5.", level: 1 },
      { q: "An algorithm is best described as", options: ["a single lucky guess", "a systematic procedure built from basic steps", "a special kind of number", "a drawing of a solution"], answer: 1, explain: "An algorithm is a systematic step-by-step procedure that uses simple operations we already know how to carry out.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Using Euclid's algorithm, gcd(84, 36) is", options: ["6", "18", "4", "12"], answer: 3, explain: "84 mod 36 = 12; 36 mod 12 = 0. The last non-zero remainder is 12, so gcd = 12.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The value of gcd(135, 225) is", options: ["15", "45", "90", "135"], answer: 1, explain: "225 mod 135 = 90; 135 mod 90 = 45; 90 mod 45 = 0. So the gcd is 45.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A floor 360 cm long and 240 cm wide is to be covered with square tiles of one size without cutting any tile.\nThe largest possible side of a tile is", options: ["60 cm", "240 cm", "120 cm", "80 cm"], answer: 2, explain: "The side must divide both 360 and 240, so the largest is gcd(360, 240). 360 mod 240 = 120; 240 mod 120 = 0, so gcd = 120 cm.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): For m ≥ n, gcd(m, n) = gcd(n, m − n).\nReason (R): A number that divides both m and n also divides m − n, and a number that divides both n and m − n also divides m.",options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 0, explain: "R shows the two pairs have exactly the same common divisors, so they have the same greatest common divisor. R is the justification of A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "Assertion (A): Adding large numbers by counting dots is more efficient than the column method.\nReason (R): In the column method, each extra digit adds only one more column of work.",options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 3, explain: "R is true, and it is why the column method is the efficient one. Counting dots needs about ten times as many dots for each extra digit, so A is false.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "The value of gcd(55, 34) is", options: ["5", "1", "11", "17"], answer: 1, explain: "55 mod 34 = 21; 34 mod 21 = 13; 21 mod 13 = 8; 13 mod 8 = 5; 8 mod 5 = 3; 5 mod 3 = 2; 3 mod 2 = 1; 2 mod 1 = 0. The gcd is 1 (consecutive Fibonacci numbers are coprime).", level: 3 },
      { q: "Two ropes of lengths 2310 cm and 1848 cm are cut into pieces of equal, greatest possible length with nothing left over.\nThe total number of pieces is", options: ["18", "4", "9", "7"], answer: 2, explain: "gcd(2310, 1848): 2310 mod 1848 = 462; 1848 mod 462 = 0, so the length is 462 cm. Pieces: 2310/462 = 5 and 1848/462 = 4, a total of 9.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Using Euclid's remainder algorithm, gcd(1071, 462) is", options: ["147", "3", "7", "21"], answer: 3, explain: "1071 mod 462 = 147; 462 mod 147 = 21; 147 mod 21 = 0. The last non-zero remainder is 21.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is an algorithm?", a: "An algorithm is a systematic, step-by-step procedure for solving a problem, built from basic steps that we already know how to do.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Find 23 mod 7.", a: "23 = 3 × 7 + 2, so 23 mod 7 = **2**.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "State the rule on which the remainder version of Euclid's algorithm is based.", a: "gcd(m, n) = gcd(n, m mod n), repeated until the remainder is 0.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Use Euclid's algorithm to find gcd(96, 60).", a: "96 mod 60 = 36.\n60 mod 36 = 24.\n36 mod 24 = 12.\n24 mod 12 = 0.\nThe last non-zero remainder gives gcd = **12**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Why is gcd(m, 0) = m?", a: "Every number divides 0, so the common divisors of m and 0 are exactly the divisors of m. The greatest of them is **m**. This is the stopping rule of Euclid's algorithm.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Find gcd(30, 18) by the subtraction method, showing each step.", a: "gcd(30, 18) = gcd(18, 12) (since 30 − 18 = 12)\n= gcd(12, 6) (18 − 12 = 6)\n= gcd(6, 6) (12 − 6 = 6)\n= gcd(6, 0) (6 − 6 = 0)\n= **6**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Find gcd(100, 45) with the remainder method.", a: "100 mod 45 = 10; 45 mod 10 = 5; 10 mod 5 = 0.\nSo gcd(100, 45) = **5**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Use Euclid's algorithm to find gcd(1260, 198).", a: "1260 = 6 × 198 + 72, so 1260 mod 198 = 72.\n198 mod 72 = 54 (198 = 2 × 72 + 54).\n72 mod 54 = 18.\n54 mod 18 = 0.\nSo gcd = **18**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain why gcd(m, n) = gcd(n, m − n) when m ≥ n.", a: "If d divides both m and n, then d divides their difference m − n; so d is a common divisor of n and m − n.\nConversely, if d divides n and m − n, then it divides their sum n + (m − n) = m; so d is a common divisor of m and n.\nThus the two pairs have **exactly the same common divisors**, hence the same greatest one.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Write Euclid's algorithm in words for finding gcd(m, n) with m ≥ n.", a: "1. Divide m by n and find the remainder r = m mod n.\n2. If r = 0, stop: the answer is n.\n3. Otherwise replace the pair (m, n) by (n, r) and repeat from step 1.\nThe last non-zero remainder (the value of n when r becomes 0) is the gcd.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A hall is 840 cm long and 588 cm wide. It is to be paved with identical square tiles, with no cutting. Find the largest possible side of a tile and the number of tiles needed.", a: "The side must divide both 840 and 588, so it is gcd(840, 588).\n840 mod 588 = 252.\n588 mod 252 = 84.\n252 mod 84 = 0.\nSo the largest side is **84 cm**.\nTiles along the length = 840/84 = 10; along the width = 588/84 = 7.\nNumber of tiles = 10 × 7 = **70**.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Find gcd(1000, 3) by the subtraction method and by the remainder method. Compare how many steps each needs.", a: "Subtraction: 1000 − 3 − 3 − … repeatedly; 1000 = 333 × 3 + 1, so about 333 subtractions are needed before the value 1 appears, and then a few more steps follow.\nRemainder: 1000 mod 3 = 1, then 3 mod 1 = 0. That is just 2 steps.\nBoth give **gcd = 1**.\nThe remainder method is far more efficient because one division does the work of hundreds of subtractions.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Find gcd(3465, 2310) by Euclid's algorithm and verify the answer using prime factorisation.", a: "3465 mod 2310 = 1155.\n2310 mod 1155 = 0.\nSo the gcd is **1155**.\nVerification: 3465 = 3² × 5 × 7 × 11 and 2310 = 2 × 3 × 5 × 7 × 11.\nCommon prime factors: 3 × 5 × 7 × 11 = 1155. ✓", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: A tailor has two ribbons, 252 cm and 198 cm long. She wants to cut both into pieces of the same greatest possible length with no ribbon left over.\n(i) What is the length of each piece?\n(ii) How many pieces does she get from each ribbon?\n(iii) How many pieces does she get in all?", a: "(i) The length is gcd(252, 198).\n252 mod 198 = 54; 198 mod 54 = 36; 54 mod 36 = 18; 36 mod 18 = 0.\nSo each piece is **18 cm**.\n(ii) 252/18 = **14** pieces and 198/18 = **11** pieces.\n(iii) Total = 14 + 11 = **25 pieces**.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "quadrilaterals": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The sum of the four interior angles of any quadrilateral is", options: ["180°", "270°", "360°", "540°"], answer: 2, explain: "A diagonal splits a quadrilateral into two triangles, each with angle sum 180°, giving 360°.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In a parallelogram, the diagonals", options: ["are always equal", "are always perpendicular", "bisect each other", "are parallel to each other"], answer: 2, explain: "The diagonals of every parallelogram bisect each other. They are equal only in a rectangle and perpendicular only in a rhombus.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In △ABC, the segment joining the midpoints of AB and AC is 9 cm long. The length of BC is", options: ["4.5 cm", "9 cm", "18 cm", "36 cm"], answer: 2, explain: "By the midpoint theorem the segment is half of BC, so BC = 2 × 9 = 18 cm.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The centroid of a triangle divides each median, from the vertex, in the ratio", options: ["1 : 1", "1 : 2", "3 : 1", "2 : 1"], answer: 3, explain: "The part from the vertex to the centroid is twice the part from the centroid to the midpoint of the opposite side.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Two adjacent angles of a parallelogram are in the ratio 2 : 3. The smaller angle is", options: ["108°", "60°", "72°", "36°"], answer: 2, explain: "Adjacent angles add to 180°: 2k + 3k = 180°, so k = 36°. The angles are 72° and 108°; the smaller is 72°.", level: 2 },
      { q: "In △ABC, AD is a median of length 18 cm and G is the centroid. The length of GD is", options: ["12 cm", "6 cm", "9 cm", "3 cm"], answer: 1, explain: "G divides AD in the ratio 2 : 1, so GD = (1/3) × 18 = 6 cm and AG = 12 cm.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A farmer has a four-sided field ABCD of any shape. She joins the midpoints of its sides, in order, with a path.\nThe path always encloses a", options: ["rectangle", "square", "parallelogram", "trapezium with only one parallel pair"], answer: 2, explain: "Joining the midpoints of the sides of any quadrilateral, in order, gives a parallelogram (Varignon's theorem). It becomes a rectangle only if the diagonals are perpendicular.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): If the diagonals of a quadrilateral bisect each other, it is a parallelogram.\nReason (R): The triangles formed by the halves of the diagonals on opposite sides of the meeting point are congruent by SAS.",options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 0, explain: "The congruent triangles give AB = CD and AB ∥ CD, so one pair of opposite sides is equal and parallel. R is the argument behind A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "Assertion (A): In a triangle, the segment joining the midpoints of two sides is equal in length to the third side.\nReason (R): The segment joining the midpoints of two sides is parallel to the third side and half as long.",options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 3, explain: "R is the midpoint theorem and is true. It also shows A is false: the segment is only half of the third side.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "In △ABC, D and E are the midpoints of AB and AC, and DE = 7 cm. If the perimeter of △ABC is 38 cm, then AB + AC is", options: ["31 cm", "19 cm", "24 cm", "28 cm"], answer: 2, explain: "BC = 2 × DE = 14 cm. AB + AC = 38 − 14 = 24 cm. The value 31 results from subtracting DE instead of BC.", level: 3 },
      { q: "In a four-sided plot ABCD, the diagonals are AC = 10 m and BD = 14 m. P, Q, R, S are the midpoints of AB, BC, CD, DA.\nThe length of fence needed all round PQRS is", options: ["12 m", "24 m", "48 m", "34 m"], answer: 1, explain: "PQ = SR = ½ AC = 5 m (midpoint theorem in △ABC and △ADC) and QR = PS = ½ BD = 7 m. Perimeter = 2(5 + 7) = 24 m.", level: 3 },
      { q: "In a parallelogram ABCD, the bisectors of ∠A and ∠B meet at P. The measure of ∠APB is", options: ["90°", "60°", "120°", "45°"], answer: 0, explain: "∠A + ∠B = 180° (adjacent angles), so ∠PAB + ∠PBA = 90°. In △APB, ∠APB = 180° − 90° = 90°.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "State the midpoint theorem.", a: "The line segment joining the midpoints of two sides of a triangle is parallel to the third side and equal to half of it.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "In a parallelogram ABCD, ∠A = 65°. What is the measure of ∠C?", a: "Opposite angles of a parallelogram are equal, so ∠C = **65°**.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Write the sum of the angles of a quadrilateral and give a reason.", a: "**360°**. A diagonal divides the quadrilateral into two triangles, each with angle sum 180°.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "In parallelogram ABCD, ∠A = (2x + 20)° and ∠B = (3x − 10)°. Find all four angles.", a: "Adjacent angles are supplementary: 2x + 20 + 3x − 10 = 180, so 5x = 170 and x = 34.\n∠A = 88°, ∠B = 102°.\nOpposite angles are equal: ∠C = **88°**, ∠D = **102°**. (Sum = 360°.)", marks: 2 },
      { q: "In △ABC, D and E are the midpoints of AB and AC and BC = 26 cm. Find DE and name the relation between DE and BC.", a: "By the midpoint theorem, DE = ½ × 26 = **13 cm**, and DE ∥ BC.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "The median BE of △ABC is 21 cm long and G is the centroid. Find BG and GE.", a: "G divides BE in the ratio 2 : 1.\nBG = (2/3) × 21 = **14 cm**.\nGE = (1/3) × 21 = **7 cm**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "State two conditions, each sufficient, for a quadrilateral to be a parallelogram.", a: "Any two of these:\n(1) Both pairs of opposite sides are equal.\n(2) Both pairs of opposite angles are equal.\n(3) The diagonals bisect each other.\n(4) One pair of opposite sides is equal and parallel.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Prove that the diagonals of a parallelogram bisect each other.", a: "Let the diagonals AC and BD of parallelogram ABCD meet at O.\nIn △AOB and △COD: AB = CD (opposite sides), ∠OAB = ∠OCD (alternate angles, AB ∥ CD) and ∠OBA = ∠ODC (alternate angles).\nSo △AOB ≅ △COD by ASA.\nHence **AO = OC and BO = OD**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "In △ABC the vertices are A (2, 6), B (−2, 0) and C (8, 0). D and E are the midpoints of AB and AC. Verify the midpoint theorem.", a: "D = ((2 − 2)/2, (6 + 0)/2) = (0, 3). E = ((2 + 8)/2, (6 + 0)/2) = (5, 3).\nD and E both have y = 3, so DE is horizontal. B and C both have y = 0, so BC is horizontal too. Hence DE ∥ BC.\nDE = 5 − 0 = 5 units; BC = 8 − (−2) = 10 units.\nSo DE = ½ BC. ✓", marks: 3 },
      { q: "In quadrilateral ABCD, ∠A = ∠C and ∠B = ∠D. Show that ABCD is a parallelogram.", a: "The angle sum is 360°: ∠A + ∠B + ∠C + ∠D = 360°.\nSince ∠A = ∠C and ∠B = ∠D, 2∠A + 2∠B = 360°, so ∠A + ∠B = 180°.\nThe interior angles ∠A and ∠B on the same side of AB add to 180°, so AD ∥ BC. Similarly ∠B + ∠C = 180° gives AB ∥ DC.\nBoth pairs of opposite sides are parallel, so ABCD is a **parallelogram**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Prove the midpoint theorem: in △ABC, if D and E are the midpoints of AB and AC, then DE ∥ BC and DE = ½ BC.", a: "Extend DE to F so that EF = DE. Join CF.\nIn △ADE and △CFE: AE = CE (E is the midpoint), ∠AED = ∠CEF (vertically opposite), DE = FE. So △ADE ≅ △CFE (SAS).\nHence CF = AD and ∠ADE = ∠CFE, so AB ∥ CF.\nSince AD = DB, we get CF = DB, and DB ∥ CF.\nSo DBCF has one pair of opposite sides equal and parallel: it is a parallelogram.\nTherefore DF ∥ BC and DF = BC. As DE = ½ DF, we get **DE ∥ BC and DE = ½ BC**.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Prove that the quadrilateral formed by joining the midpoints of the sides of any quadrilateral, in order, is a parallelogram.", a: "Let P, Q, R, S be the midpoints of the sides AB, BC, CD and DA of quadrilateral ABCD. Join the diagonal AC.\nIn △ABC, P and Q are midpoints of AB and BC, so PQ ∥ AC and PQ = ½ AC.\nIn △ADC, S and R are midpoints of AD and DC, so SR ∥ AC and SR = ½ AC.\nHence PQ ∥ SR and PQ = SR.\nOne pair of opposite sides is equal and parallel, so **PQRS is a parallelogram**.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "In △ABC the medians AD, BE and CF meet at the centroid G. AD = 18 cm, BE = 24 cm and CF = 30 cm. Find AG, GD, BG, GE, CG and GF, and the sum AG + BG + CG.", a: "G divides each median in the ratio 2 : 1 from the vertex.\nAG = (2/3) × 18 = **12 cm**, GD = (1/3) × 18 = **6 cm**.\nBG = (2/3) × 24 = **16 cm**, GE = (1/3) × 24 = **8 cm**.\nCG = (2/3) × 30 = **20 cm**, GF = (1/3) × 30 = **10 cm**.\nAG + BG + CG = 12 + 16 + 20 = **48 cm** (two-thirds of 18 + 24 + 30 = 72).", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: A park is shaped like a parallelogram ABCD with ∠A = 75°. Its diagonals AC = 40 m and BD = 30 m meet at O. A path PQ joins the midpoints P of AB and Q of AD.\n(i) Find ∠B and ∠C.\n(ii) Find AO and BO.\n(iii) Find the length of the path PQ and say how it is placed relative to BD.", a: "(i) Adjacent angles are supplementary: ∠B = 180° − 75° = **105°**. Opposite angles are equal: ∠C = ∠A = **75°**.\n(ii) The diagonals bisect each other: AO = 40/2 = **20 m** and BO = 30/2 = **15 m**.\n(iii) In △ABD, P and Q are midpoints of AB and AD, so PQ = ½ BD = **15 m**, and PQ is **parallel to BD** (midpoint theorem).", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "two-variables-one-line": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The number of solutions of the equation 3x + 2y = 12 is", options: ["one", "two", "exactly six", "infinitely many"], answer: 3, explain: "For each value of x there is a matching value of y, so the equation has infinitely many solutions, all lying on one line.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which of these is a solution of x + 2y = 8?", options: ["(2, 2)", "(6, 2)", "(1, 4)", "(4, 2)"], answer: 3, explain: "For (4, 2): 4 + 2 × 2 = 8. The others give 6, 10 and 9.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The graph of a linear equation ax + by + c = 0 (a, b not both zero) is", options: ["a parabola", "a straight line", "a circle", "a pair of points"], answer: 1, explain: "Every solution is a point on one straight line, and every point on that line is a solution.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The solution of the pair x + y = 5 and x − y = 1 is", options: ["(2, 3)", "(4, 1)", "(3, 2)", "(1, 4)"], answer: 2, explain: "Adding the equations gives 2x = 6, so x = 3, and then y = 2. Check: 3 + 2 = 5 and 3 − 2 = 1.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The slope of the line 2x + 3y = 6 is", options: ["2/3", "−2/3", "−3/2", "2"], answer: 1, explain: "Rewrite as y = −(2/3)x + 2. The slope is the coefficient of x, −2/3.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The pair of equations 2x + 3y = 5 and 4x + 6y = 11 has", options: ["a unique solution", "infinitely many solutions", "no solution", "exactly two solutions"], answer: 2, explain: "a₁/a₂ = 2/4 = 1/2, b₁/b₂ = 3/6 = 1/2, but c₁/c₂ = 5/11 ≠ 1/2. The lines are parallel, so there is no solution.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Ravi buys 2 pens and 3 notebooks for ₹70. Meena buys 1 pen and 2 notebooks of the same kinds for ₹40.\nThe price of one pen is", options: ["₹25", "₹20", "₹10", "₹15"], answer: 1, explain: "2p + 3n = 70 and p + 2n = 40. From the second, p = 40 − 2n; then 80 − 4n + 3n = 70 gives n = 10 and p = 20. Check: 40 + 30 = 70.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): The pair x + y = 3 and 2x + 2y = 6 has infinitely many solutions.\nReason (R): Both equations represent the same line.",options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 0, explain: "The second equation is the first multiplied by 2, so the two lines coincide and every point of the line is a solution. R is the reason for A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "Assertion (A): The lines y = 2x + 1 and y = 2x + 5 have a common solution.\nReason (R): Lines with the same slope and different y-intercepts are parallel.",options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 3, explain: "R is true. Because the lines are parallel they never meet, so no point satisfies both equations and A is false.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "The value of k for which 2x + ky = 5 and 3x + 6y = 8 have no solution is", options: ["9", "1/4", "3", "4"], answer: 3, explain: "For no solution a₁/a₂ = b₁/b₂ ≠ c₁/c₂. 2/3 = k/6 gives k = 4, and c₁/c₂ = 5/8 ≠ 2/3, so k = 4 works.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A father is 4 times as old as his son. After 6 years he will be 3 times as old as his son.\nThe father's present age is", options: ["36 years", "40 years", "48 years", "44 years"], answer: 2, explain: "Let the son be s: father = 4s. After 6 years: 4s + 6 = 3(s + 6) = 3s + 18, so s = 12 and the father is 48. Check: 54 = 3 × 18.", level: 3 },
      { q: "The lines x + y = 7 and 2x − y = 2 meet at a point. The area of the triangle formed by the two lines and the x-axis is", options: ["24", "14", "12", "10"], answer: 2, explain: "Adding the equations: 3x = 9, so x = 3 and y = 4. The lines cut the x-axis at (7, 0) and (1, 0). Area = ½ × (7 − 1) × 4 = 12 square units.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Write the standard form of a linear equation in two variables.", a: "**ax + by + c = 0**, where a, b, c are real numbers and a, b are not both zero.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Is (1, 2) a solution of 3x + 2y = 7?", a: "3(1) + 2(2) = 3 + 4 = 7. Yes, **(1, 2) is a solution**.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What does it mean for the graphs of a pair of equations to be parallel lines, as far as solutions are concerned?", a: "The lines never meet, so the pair has **no solution**.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Find three solutions of the equation 2x + y = 6.", a: "y = 6 − 2x.\nx = 0 gives y = 6; x = 1 gives y = 4; x = 3 gives y = 0.\nThree solutions: **(0, 6), (1, 4) and (3, 0)** (others are possible).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Write 3x − 2y = 12 in the form y = mx + d. State the slope and the y-intercept.", a: "−2y = 12 − 3x, so y = (3/2)x − 6.\nSlope = **3/2**; y-intercept = **−6**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Solve x + y = 14 and x − y = 4.", a: "Adding: 2x = 18, so x = 9.\nThen y = 14 − 9 = 5.\nCheck: 9 − 5 = 4. The solution is **x = 9, y = 5**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Without solving, say how many solutions the pair 2x − y = 3 and 4x − 2y = 6 has. Give a reason.", a: "a₁/a₂ = 2/4 = 1/2, b₁/b₂ = −1/−2 = 1/2 and c₁/c₂ = −3/−6 = 1/2.\nAll three ratios are equal, so the lines coincide and there are **infinitely many solutions**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Solve by substitution: 3x + 2y = 11 and x − y = 2.", a: "From x − y = 2: x = y + 2.\nSubstitute: 3(y + 2) + 2y = 11, so 5y + 6 = 11 and y = 1.\nThen x = 3.\nCheck: 3(3) + 2(1) = 11. Solution: **x = 3, y = 1**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Solve by elimination: 2x + 3y = 13 and 5x − 2y = 4.", a: "Multiply the first equation by 2 and the second by 3:\n4x + 6y = 26 and 15x − 6y = 12.\nAdd: 19x = 38, so x = 2.\nThen 4 + 3y = 13 gives y = 3.\nCheck: 5(2) − 2(3) = 4. Solution: **x = 2, y = 3**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "For what values of k does the pair kx + 2y = 5 and 3x + y = 1 have a unique solution?", a: "For a unique solution a₁/a₂ ≠ b₁/b₂.\nk/3 ≠ 2/1, so k ≠ 6.\nThe pair has a unique solution for **every real k except 6**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Solve graphically 2x + y = 8 and x − y = 1. Write a table of three points for each line.", a: "2x + y = 8: (0, 8), (2, 4), (4, 0).\nx − y = 1: (0, −1), (1, 0), (3, 2).\nPlot the points and draw each line with a ruler.\nThe lines cross at (3, 2).\nCheck: 2(3) + 2 = 8 and 3 − 2 = 1.\nSolution: **x = 3, y = 2**.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "The sum of the digits of a two-digit number is 9. If the digits are reversed, the new number is 27 more than the original. Find the number.", a: "Let the tens digit be x and the units digit y. Original = 10x + y.\nx + y = 9 …(1)\nReversed number = 10y + x, and (10y + x) − (10x + y) = 27, so 9(y − x) = 27 and y − x = 3 …(2)\nAdd (1) and (2): 2y = 12, so y = 6 and x = 3.\nThe number is **36**.\nCheck: 63 − 36 = 27 and 3 + 6 = 9.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Consider the pair 3x + 2y = 7 and 6x + 4y = k.\n(i) For what value of k does the pair have infinitely many solutions?\n(ii) For what values of k does it have no solution?\n(iii) Can it ever have a unique solution? Explain.", a: "Here a₁/a₂ = 3/6 = 1/2 and b₁/b₂ = 2/4 = 1/2, so the lines always have the same slope.\n(i) Infinitely many solutions when c₁/c₂ = 7/k = 1/2, i.e. **k = 14**.\n(ii) No solution when 7/k ≠ 1/2, i.e. **for every k ≠ 14** (for example k = 10).\n(iii) **No.** A unique solution needs a₁/a₂ ≠ b₁/b₂, but these ratios are always equal.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: At a cinema, 3 adult tickets and 2 child tickets cost ₹1100, while 2 adult tickets and 3 child tickets cost ₹1000.\n(i) Write the two equations, taking the price of an adult ticket as ₹a and a child ticket as ₹c.\n(ii) Find a and c.\n(iii) What is the cost of 4 adult and 4 child tickets?", a: "(i) 3a + 2c = 1100 and 2a + 3c = 1000.\n(ii) Multiply the first by 3 and the second by 2: 9a + 6c = 3300 and 4a + 6c = 2000.\nSubtract: 5a = 1300, so **a = 260**.\nThen 2c = 1100 − 780 = 320, so **c = 160**.\nCheck: 2(260) + 3(160) = 520 + 480 = 1000.\n(iii) 4(260) + 4(160) = 1040 + 640 = **₹1680**.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "math-of-space-surface-area-and-volume": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The volume of a cube of side 5 cm is", options: ["25 cm³", "125 cm³", "150 cm³", "75 cm³"], answer: 1, explain: "V = a³ = 5 × 5 × 5 = 125 cm³. The value 150 is the total surface area, 6a².", level: 1 },
      { q: "The curved surface area of a cylinder of radius 14 cm and height 10 cm (π = 22/7) is", options: ["880 cm²", "440 cm²", "6160 cm²", "308 cm²"], answer: 0, explain: "CSA = 2πrh = 2 × (22/7) × 14 × 10 = 880 cm². The value 6160 is the volume πr²h.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The total surface area of a solid hemisphere of radius 7 cm (π = 22/7) is", options: ["308 cm²", "462 cm²", "154 cm²", "616 cm²"], answer: 1, explain: "TSA = 3πr² = 3 × (22/7) × 49 = 462 cm². The curved part alone is 2πr² = 308 cm², and 616 cm² is the area of a full sphere.", level: 1 },
      { q: "A cone has base radius 6 cm and height 8 cm. Its slant height is", options: ["14 cm", "10 cm", "2 cm", "√28 cm"], answer: 1, explain: "l² = r² + h² = 36 + 64 = 100, so l = 10 cm.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The volume of a cone of base radius 6 cm and height 7 cm (π = 22/7) is", options: ["792 cm³", "264 cm³", "528 cm³", "132 cm³"], answer: 1, explain: "V = ⅓πr²h = ⅓ × (22/7) × 36 × 7 = ⅓ × 792 = 264 cm³. The value 792 forgets the factor ⅓.", level: 2 },
      { q: "The volume of a sphere of diameter 21 cm (π = 22/7) is", options: ["38808 cm³", "9702 cm³", "4851 cm³", "1617 cm³"], answer: 2, explain: "r = 10.5 cm. V = (4/3)(22/7)(10.5)³ = (4/3)(22/7)(1157.625) = 4851 cm³. The value 38808 uses the diameter 21 as the radius.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A water tank is a cuboid 3 m long, 2 m wide and 1.5 m high.\nIts capacity in litres is", options: ["900 L", "9000 L", "90000 L", "90 L"], answer: 1, explain: "Volume = 3 × 2 × 1.5 = 9 m³. Since 1 m³ = 1000 L, the capacity is 9000 L.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Assertion (A): The volume of a cone is one-third of the volume of a cylinder with the same base and height.\nReason (R): The volume of the cone is ⅓πr²h while that of the cylinder is πr²h.",options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 0, explain: "Dividing ⅓πr²h by πr²h gives ⅓, so R states exactly the comparison made in A.", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "Assertion (A): The total surface area of a solid hemisphere of radius r is 2πr².\nReason (R): The curved surface of the hemisphere is 2πr², and the flat circular face adds a further πr².",options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."], answer: 3, explain: "R is true. It shows the total is 2πr² + πr² = 3πr², so A is false (2πr² is only the curved surface).", level: 2, keepOrder: true },
      { q: "A toy is a cone of height 7 cm placed on a hemisphere of radius 7 cm, with the same base radius (π = 22/7).\nThe volume of the toy is", options: ["1437 cm³", "1796 cm³", "359 cm³", "1078 cm³"], answer: 3, explain: "Hemisphere = ⅔πr³ and cone = ⅓πr²h, so V = π(⅔ × 343 + ⅓ × 49 × 7) = π × 343 = (22/7) × 343 = 1078 cm³. The other values are close to the full sphere (≈1437), sphere plus cone (≈1796) and cone alone (≈359).", level: 3 },
      { q: "A cylindrical tank has radius 7 m and height 10 m. Its curved surface is to be painted at ₹20 per m² (π = 22/7).\nThe cost of painting is", options: ["₹14,960", "₹8,800", "₹30,800", "₹3,080"], answer: 1, explain: "CSA = 2πrh = 2 × (22/7) × 7 × 10 = 440 m². Cost = 440 × 20 = ₹8,800. ₹14,960 would include both circular ends and ₹30,800 would use the volume.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A solid metal cone of base radius 6 cm and height 8 cm is melted and recast into small solid spheres of radius 2 cm. The number of spheres formed is", options: ["6", "12", "9", "18"], answer: 2, explain: "Cone volume = ⅓π(36)(8) = 96π cm³. Sphere volume = (4/3)π(8) = 32π/3 cm³. Number = 96π ÷ (32π/3) = 9.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Find the total surface area of a cube of side 6 cm.", a: "TSA = 6a² = 6 × 36 = **216 cm²**.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Find the volume of a cuboid measuring 5 cm × 4 cm × 3 cm.", a: "V = lwh = 5 × 4 × 3 = **60 cm³**.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Write the relation between the radius r, height h and slant height l of a right circular cone.", a: "**l² = r² + h²**.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "A cylinder has radius 7 cm and height 15 cm. Find its curved surface area and volume. (π = 22/7)", a: "CSA = 2πrh = 2 × (22/7) × 7 × 15 = **660 cm²**.\nV = πr²h = (22/7) × 49 × 15 = **2310 cm³**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A cone has base radius 21 cm and slant height 29 cm. Find its height and curved surface area. (π = 22/7)", a: "h = √(l² − r²) = √(841 − 441) = √400 = **20 cm**.\nCSA = πrl = (22/7) × 21 × 29 = 22 × 3 × 29 = **1914 cm²**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Find the surface area and the volume of a sphere of diameter 21 cm. (π = 22/7)", a: "r = 10.5 cm.\nSA = 4πr² = 4 × (22/7) × 110.25 = **1386 cm²**.\nV = (4/3)πr³ = (4/3) × (22/7) × 1157.625 = **4851 cm³**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Find the curved surface area and the total surface area of a solid hemisphere of radius 21 cm. (π = 22/7)", a: "CSA = 2πr² = 2 × (22/7) × 441 = **2772 cm²**.\nTSA = 3πr² = 3 × (22/7) × 441 = **4158 cm²**.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "A closed rectangular box is 40 cm long, 30 cm wide and 20 cm high. Find its total surface area and its capacity in litres.", a: "TSA = 2(lw + wh + hl) = 2(1200 + 600 + 800) = 2 × 2600 = **5200 cm²**.\nV = 40 × 30 × 20 = 24000 cm³.\nSince 1 L = 1000 cm³, capacity = **24 litres**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A well of diameter 14 m is dug 20 m deep. The earth taken out is spread evenly over a rectangular field 55 m by 28 m. Find the height of the raised field. (π = 22/7)", a: "Radius = 7 m. Volume of earth = πr²h = (22/7) × 49 × 20 = 3080 m³.\nBase area of the field = 55 × 28 = 1540 m².\nHeight = 3080 ÷ 1540 = **2 m**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A cylinder and a cone have the same base radius 7 cm and the same height 12 cm. Find the volume of each and the ratio of their volumes. (π = 22/7)", a: "Cylinder: V = πr²h = (22/7) × 49 × 12 = **1848 cm³**.\nCone: V = ⅓πr²h = 1848 ÷ 3 = **616 cm³**.\nRatio cylinder : cone = 1848 : 616 = **3 : 1**.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "A tent has a cylindrical lower part of radius 14 m and height 3 m, topped by a conical roof of the same radius with slant height 18 m. Find the area of canvas needed and its cost at ₹50 per m². (π = 22/7)", a: "Curved surface of the cylinder = 2πrh = 2 × (22/7) × 14 × 3 = 264 m².\nCurved surface of the cone = πrl = (22/7) × 14 × 18 = 792 m².\nCanvas needed = 264 + 792 = **1056 m²** (the base is not covered).\nCost = 1056 × 50 = **₹52,800**.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "A hemispherical bowl has an inner radius of 10.5 cm. (π = 22/7)\nFind (i) its capacity in cm³ and in litres, (ii) the area of its inner curved surface, (iii) the height of water in a cylinder of base radius 7 cm if the full bowl is emptied into it.", a: "(i) V = ⅔πr³ = ⅔ × (22/7) × 1157.625 = **2425.5 cm³** = **2.4255 litres**.\n(ii) Inner curved surface = 2πr² = 2 × (22/7) × 110.25 = **693 cm²**.\n(iii) Base area of the cylinder = (22/7) × 49 = 154 cm².\nHeight = 2425.5 ÷ 154 = **15.75 cm**.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Three solid metal cubes of edges 3 cm, 4 cm and 5 cm are melted and recast into a single cube. Find the edge of the new cube, and compare its surface area with the sum of the surface areas of the three cubes.", a: "Total volume = 27 + 64 + 125 = 216 cm³.\nNew edge = ∛216 = **6 cm**.\nSurface area of the new cube = 6 × 36 = **216 cm²**.\nSum of the original surface areas = 6(9 + 16 + 25) = 6 × 50 = 300 cm².\nThe new surface area is 300 − 216 = **84 cm² less**; the volume is conserved but the surface area is not.", marks: 5, type: "long" },
      { q: "Case study: A town has a cylindrical water tank of radius 7 m and height 4 m. (π = 22/7)\n(i) Find the volume of the tank in m³.\n(ii) How many litres of water does it hold when full?\n(iii) A pump fills it at 11,000 litres per minute. How long does it take to fill the empty tank?", a: "(i) V = πr²h = (22/7) × 49 × 4 = **616 m³**.\n(ii) 1 m³ = 1000 L, so capacity = **616,000 litres**.\n(iii) Time = 616,000 ÷ 11,000 = **56 minutes**.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
});
